response:
In fields where sunbeams dance and sway,
A harvest waits, to soon decay;
Golden strands that rustle with the breeze,
Dry up to form a farmer's prize: sweet hay.

A fragrance fills the air so rare,
As farmers rake the bales with care;
The scent of summer's golden gleam,
Mixed with the earthy musk of farming's dream.

In barns, these bundles serve as food,
For cows and horses, strong and good;
A source of nourishment, for animals,
To grow and thrive beneath its gentle raiments.

The sight of hay is peaceful, calm;
A symphony of browns and golds, entwined;
A scene of tranquil rustic charm,
That warms the heart, and eases troubled minds.

And thus, this humble straw we see,
A symbol of the land, and all it be;
It feeds our creatures, and our souls as well,
A simple gift, that nature does compel.
